00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
even for the most book obsessed of us  once life starts getting in the way  it becomes very difficult and almost  impossible  to keep up both with a growing book wish  list and with a growing task list  and this has definitely been the case  for me my name is elizabeth i'm a  medical student and an artist living in  london  and this year for a while i managed to  read about a book a day even though it  was one of the busiest years of my life  the reason i was able to do this was  because i completely changed the way  that i read and what i used to read so i  just wanted to  share my method in case you are also  interested in reading more  the main change that i did was going  from physical books to  an app called speechify speedify is an  app that lets you upload any sort of  text and it will read it out loud to you  in this video i will go through exactly  how to set things up in the app and how  it works  and perhaps more importantly exactly why  this is so game changing and how this  can completely  overthrow the way that you read and  consume books the way that you use  beautify is that you just download the  app and then you can upload any sort of  text  this can be a copy pasteable text a pdf  an epub  ibook anything basically can be uploaded  to it  and in only a few seconds it will  convert it into  an audio file the beautiful thing about  it is that you don't only have the voice  you can listen to  but also you will have the text  scrolling itself automatically through  the screen  and a highlighter going through the two  or three words that are being said at a  time  depending on your speed you can then  basically just have the app read the  book aloud to you  you can very easily adjust the speed at  which you listen to the book  and therefore you have a free audiobook  we'll bookmark things for you  automatically so you can always go back  to exactly where you left off and it  also tells you how long you have left in  the book in terms of time so that is how  you set up speechify and how it works so  it's fairly straightforward now i will  go through exactly how it changed my  reading  and why i'm able to basically read so  much if i want to first and most  significant thing i think is  speed the speed can be adjusted very  very easily i started  at around the mid 600s which i would say  is average um listening speed when i  first downloaded the app but i've been  using it consistently for months now and  at this point i am quite comfortable  with the 900 speed this is 900 words per  minute so you can go through a book very  very fast  for reference most of the books that i  read would be around one hour to two  hours with a maximum being four hours  for a very very  long book if it takes an hour and  something to read a book that's really  not a significant amount of time and you  can definitely go through one every  single day  um not that there's any reason to do  this but if you wanted to i will just  have to comment a bit on what my mom  says a lot  which is shouldn't you read books slowly  and enjoy them i  fully agree with this um i would never  use this method or read  books for fun at the speed i still use  physical books for things like virginia  woolf or  things that i read for fun which is  mostly fiction i will read very very  slowly  in a week or two and really take my time  and enjoy it  um but alongside these when there are  informational books i will read them  very very fast  and i i feel that i have some reasons to  do this um  firstly i think that informational books  have a lot of fluff  so authors will really over explain  things just to make a single point  and i feel that it's quite unnecessary  to go through the whole book at the same  level of detail or be limited by my  speed for most of the book  so when i listen to a book in speechify  i will hear it at maximum speed and then  when there's something very interesting  or a very good point being made  i will slow it right down or completely  pause the app and take my time to think  about what i've just read  and take notes on it so this way i'm  very empowered and i'm in control of  how interesting i find the book rather  than the word count the author had to  hit for example  my second comment on the speed is well  it is worth if you read  things so fast surely you must forget  all of them and  i agree with this yes i do forget most  of them but the thing is i always used  to  even if i read a book in like a month if  you would ask me half a year later what  i remember  it would not be a lot it would just be  the things i took notes on and i would  definitely need refreshers i would not  remember a lot from that book  anyway and i feel that it's the exact  same thing with speechify  so even though i read books so so fast i  still pause at the things i want to  remember  so i remember them the same amount if i  read them very very slowly or if i read  them extremely fast  so in the end of the day i don't think  that there is a significant difference  in terms of retention for me in relation  to how fast i read the book the next  benefit to using an app like speechify  is the fact that it is digital  and as you can see i do have a lot of  physical books and  i really i love them um i think there's  something really romantic  and nice to just have a paper book and  lie in bed and read it or take physical  notes which i also used to do a lot  and i was quite snobbish when it came to  ebooks before i  really hated them and you can have a lot  of benefit from having electronic books  the first being that you can read them  absolutely everywhere i do try to keep a  paper back  in my bag all the time but there are  places where  i can't use a book but i almost always  have my phone on me  so it's very convenient to have an app  on my phone if you read digital books  you can very easily copy and paste  extracts from the book online already so  i have all my book notes in notion so  they're all in one place  and i used to find so i used to take  physical notes  which looked like this so i have  notebooks and notebooks full of physical  notes that i used to take from books  i find this very very fun the thing is  it's just here in my bedroom and nowhere  else and if i'm having a talk with  someone and i think oh my god there was  this quote in this book that would be so  great to mention right now or  i really want to tell you about the  study i can't find it and it's sometimes  such a hassle  or completely impossible to find that  book extract just by googling it  so by having things on notion it's so  much easier and almost guaranteed that i  can find the resource that i need when i  need it  so i find that very very useful beyond  that and what is very important to me  is that it's so much cheaper i've spent  so much money on books and they're  really really expensive  as a student wanting to buy a book or  two or three a week  just really adds up they're like 10  pounds and above for a book so it's  quite a significant amount of money  and speechify there's so many different  plans i'm on the 40 pounds a year plan  and beyond that i don't need to pay for  anything else there's so many resources  to find free books online  and also a lot of authors give their  books online for free if you just sign  up to their newsletters so  honestly most of the books i want to  ever read i can find them online for  free anyway  so i could just download them upload  them to speechify and  listen to them that way without  basically spending any money um  and yes as a student this is a very  significant plus for me  my last point will be on speechify  versus audible  now i feel a certain way about audible i  do think that  it's honestly the gold standard when it  comes to listening to books uh because  they are actually narrated by people and  not ai machines  um nothing sounds like it they're  absolutely brilliant it's very very nice  however it's so expensive the  subscription itself is quite expensive  and it only covers one book a month  so if you want to buy another book you  would have to pay for that too  um and that adds up quite a lot the  thing that really annoys me  with audible is that they don't have the  text on the screen so if i want to take  a note  i would have to type it out go backwards  and forwards the last 15 seconds to make  sure i got every word right  and i don't have a physical copy of the  book anywhere and it's just so much  hassle  while with speechify because the text is  always on the screen i can always just  like highlight it  copy paste it and take it elsewhere and  this is so so important so i think  if i were to get free endless audible  books i probably still use speechify to  listen to non-fiction books but i would  use audible for fiction books if that  makes sense  not at four times the speed if you  haven't heard or tried speed to five  before i would definitely definitely  definitely download it it is such a good  app  you can get a free trial so you can see  if it works for you anyway if you have a  lot to read from university but you  can't be bothered to do it it's nice  just to upload it lie in bed and let  someone  read it out to you if you find that  books are too expensive this is a way to  kind of  get around that and find it for a bit  cheaper you're trying to train yourself  to read faster for whatever reason this  is a brilliant way to do that as always  if you made it to this point  thank you so much for watching i hope  you have a wonderful rest of your day  be kind to yourself and others and don't  believe everything you think thanks bye

